1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,820
Does a triangle have just one center, and how
can we partition terrain with an inward notch?

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,440
We will observe real game tiles and terrain,

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:11,080
then distinguish the questions answered by
centroid, incenter and circumcenter.

4
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,140
Next we will validate polygon boundaries and
convexity,

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:17,480
and examine the limits of a triangle fan.

6
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:21,100
Finally we will partition a concave example by
ear

7
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:24,620
clipping and check coverage and thin
triangles.

8
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:28,690
In Dorfromantik, hexagonal tiles rotate and
enter empty positions.

9
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:31,510
Observe each tile center and outer edges
together.

10
00:00:31,930 --> 00:00:36,450
Placing a central marker and fitting an
interior circle ask different questions.

11
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:39,710
The center of a circle through the corners
answers another question.

12
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:42,410
Shape can change all three answers.

13
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:46,150
Symmetric regular hexagons make centers appear
coincident;

14
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:49,270
an arbitrary triangle need not do so.

15
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:52,530
Connected tiles change the entire land
outline.

16
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:56,010
Distinguish one tile from the combined region.

17
00:00:56,430 --> 00:00:59,750
We will compare a three-four-five triangle in
our own defined example,

18
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:04,250
without inferring the game implementation.

19
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:10,480
The centroid is the average of three triangle
vertices and the intersection of the medians.

20
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,000
The incenter is equally distant from the three

21
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:16,160
sides and centers a circle tangent inside
them.

22
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,980
The circumcenter is equally distant from the

23
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,820
three vertices and centers a circle through
them.

24
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,760
Coordinate average, distance to sides,

25
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,240
and distance to vertices: decide what should
be equal first.

26
00:01:28,381 --> 00:01:31,960
Compare the right triangle with vertices
zero-zero,

27
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,800
four-zero and zero-three on the same diagram.

28
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:37,420
The three centers differ here.

29
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:40,820
Their shared name does not make their roles
interchangeable.

30
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:46,970
Add the three vertices and divide by three.
Our centroid is four thirds, one.

31
00:01:47,210 --> 00:01:50,530
A median joins a vertex to the opposite
midpoint;

32
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:54,110
the centroid lies two thirds along it from the
vertex.

33
00:01:54,550 --> 00:02:00,130
The other medians meet the same point. Each
vertex has barycentric weight one third.

34
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:04,270
It is also the mass center of a uniform
triangular lamina.

35
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:07,610
Nonuniform density requires a different
calculation.

36
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:12,970
For a polygon partition, weight triangle
centroids by area.

37
00:02:13,190 --> 00:02:16,070
Averaging polygon vertices generally gives the
wrong area centroid.

38
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:18,810
Collinear vertices still have a coordinate
average but no

39
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:24,130
nonzero-area triangular lamina. Validate the
input separately.

40
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,940
In Serious Sam2, observe the walkable surface

41
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:31,580
and edges while moving between paths and
bridges.

42
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:38,100
A marker location and clearance from
boundaries are different geometric problems.

43
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:44,260
For a defined triangular platform, the vertex
average can be one marker reference.

44
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:52,300
It does not have equal distances to all sides.
Circular clearance needs side distances.

45
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,740
Actual paths can have complex outlines.

46
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:59,300
Replacing them with one triangle loses
boundaries.

47
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,520
Our next incenter example verifies equal side
distances

48
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:07,760
numerically rather than claiming the game
collision method.

49
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,650
Angle bisectors meet at the incenter.

50
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:15,570
Weight each vertex by the length of its
opposite side.

51
00:03:15,910 --> 00:03:22,930
The opposite lengths are five, three and four,
with perimeter twelve.

52
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:25,730
Divide the weighted sum by twelve to obtain
one-one,

53
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:28,590
at distance one from both coordinate axes.

54
00:03:28,970 --> 00:03:32,590
The hypotenuse is three x plus four y minus
twelve equals zero.

55
00:03:32,790 --> 00:03:36,030
At the incenter, its absolute residual divided
by five is also one.

56
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:39,250
The inradius is twice area divided by
perimeter:

57
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:42,550
twice six divided by twelve equals one.

58
00:03:42,730 --> 00:03:45,010
Equivalently divide area by semiperimeter.

59
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:49,570
Confusing perimeter with semiperimeter
produces half the correct radius.

60
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:54,420
Intersect perpendicular bisectors of two sides

61
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:56,960
to find a center equally distant from the
vertices.

62
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:00,840
Midpoints two-zero and zero-one point five
give

63
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:05,440
intersecting bisectors at two-one point five.

64
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,600
Its distance from the origin is two point
five,

65
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:12,760
equal to the other vertex distances: the
circumradius.

66
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,780
A right triangle places the center at the
hypotenuse midpoint;

67
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:20,500
an acute triangle places it inside and an
obtuse triangle outside.

68
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:25,180
The obtuse triangle zero-zero, four-zero,
one-one has circumcenter two-minus one,

69
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:28,700
outside but still satisfying the condition.

70
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:32,660
Collinear points do not determine a unique
finite circumcircle;

71
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:36,340
nearly collinear inputs are numerically
unstable.

72
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:40,190
In Noita, observe protrusions and inward

73
00:04:40,450 --> 00:04:43,190
notches while moving through irregular
terrain.

74
00:04:43,270 --> 00:04:47,010
A polygon records vertices in boundary order.

75
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:50,610
Reordering the same points changes the shape.

76
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:56,070
A shortcut across an inward boundary can
incorrectly fill a passage or empty region.

77
00:04:56,450 --> 00:05:02,230
Repeated points and tiny edges can destabilize
direction and area calculations.

78
00:05:02,290 --> 00:05:05,870
We use visible contours to motivate valid
input,

79
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:09,130
without asserting a triangle mesh
implementation for pixel terrain.

80
00:05:09,610 --> 00:05:13,250
Our next diagram defines a simple six-vertex
concave

81
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:16,210
polygon and checks its ordered boundary and
missing region.

82
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:20,920
A polygon boundary closes from its last vertex
to its first;

83
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,960
store vertices in boundary order.

84
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:31,280
Our L runs through zero-zero, four-zero,
four-one, one-one, one-four, zero-four.

85
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:36,800
The upper-right missing region is outside;
one-one is the concave vertex.

86
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:42,900
Check finite coordinates, duplicate points,
zero-length edges and zero-area input first.

87
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:48,980
For3D vertices, check coplanarity and
intersections of nonadjacent edges as well.

88
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:53,100
We partition a simple polygon without holes or
self-intersections.

89
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:56,620
Holes require additional contours and a
suitable algorithm.

90
00:05:57,250 --> 00:06:01,890
A convex region contains the entire segment
between

91
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:04,570
every two of its points; it has no inward
notch.

92
00:06:04,810 --> 00:06:07,070
A regular hexagon and a triangle are convex:

93
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:10,650
interior point pairs stay inside when
connected.

94
00:06:10,770 --> 00:06:13,170
In our L, a segment from upper-left to
lower-right

95
00:06:13,170 --> 00:06:16,690
crosses the missing upper-right region.

96
00:06:17,050 --> 00:06:22,770
Its endpoints are inside but part of the
segment is outside, proving concavity.

97
00:06:22,770 --> 00:06:29,210
For a valid simple planar boundary, consistent
turn orientation provides another check.

98
00:06:29,410 --> 00:06:33,330
Do not apply a turn test blindly to a
self-intersecting contour.

99
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:36,110
Validate order and intersections first.

100
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:40,780
Adding Dorfromantik hexagons changes the land
boundary.

101
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:44,420
Distinguish one tile from the entire connected
region.

102
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:49,220
An individual tile is convex while its union
can contain inward notches.

103
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:55,960
Connecting two land positions can cross an
empty tile location.

104
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:02,160
A union of convex pieces is not necessarily
convex; test its complete boundary separately.

105
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:08,780
An area with holes needs inner exclusion
contours in addition to the outer boundary.

106
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:15,680
We will examine whole-shape convexity and why
an angle sum alone cannot distinguish it.

107
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:20,430
Inspect the2D cross sign of consecutive edges.

108
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:25,650
With counterclockwise winding, convex vertices
turn left.

109
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:32,010
The L notch turns right, mixing turn signs in
this valid simple example.

110
00:07:32,230 --> 00:07:35,910
Collinear triples have zero cross product.

111
00:07:35,910 --> 00:07:39,070
Define strict convexity or a policy allowing
collinear points.

112
00:07:39,470 --> 00:07:43,370
Every simple polygon has interior-angle sum n
minus

113
00:07:43,370 --> 00:07:46,970
two times180 degrees, whether convex or
concave.

114
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:53,110
Six vertices give720 degrees. A correct angle
sum alone does not prove convexity.

115
00:07:53,387 --> 00:07:59,630
For acos, reject zero edges and clamp its
input to minus one through one.

116
00:07:59,830 --> 00:08:02,530
The smaller unsigned angle does not identify a
reflex interior angle.

117
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,980
For a convex polygon, diagonals from the first
vertex can connect to the remaining vertices.

118
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:12,220
Emit triangles zero-one-two, then
zero-two-three,

119
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:15,160
advancing while sharing the first vertex.

120
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:21,160
Six vertices produce four triangles, generally
n minus two.

121
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:24,980
Convexity keeps the diagonals and triangles
inside.

122
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:27,760
This condition makes the simple fan safe.

123
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:34,440
Keep consistent winding and reference original
vertex indices for positions and attributes.

124
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:37,920
The correct triangle count alone does not
prove a valid partition;

125
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:40,800
verify coverage without overlap or gaps.

126
00:08:41,290 --> 00:08:47,750
Observe the open notches and terrain edges as
the Noita character traverses them.

127
00:08:47,850 --> 00:08:53,310
Segments from one boundary vertex to every
other vertex may cross empty space.

128
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:58,150
Using such segments as triangle edges can fill
land that did not exist.

129
00:08:58,410 --> 00:09:04,170
Other triangles may overlap. Triangle count
alone misses both errors.

130
00:09:04,410 --> 00:09:10,630
This illustrates a faulty geometric
construction, not an observed bug in the game.

131
00:09:10,630 --> 00:09:15,950
We will change the fan anchor on our L to draw
a concrete failure.

132
00:09:16,687 --> 00:09:21,680
Choose four-one as the L fan anchor and

133
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:26,960
emit four triangles in boundary order.

134
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:33,320
Some triangles toward one-four and zero-four
extend into the missing region.

135
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:39,420
Signed triangle areas can still sum to seven
because erroneous contributions cancel.

136
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:44,740
The L area is also seven, but matching totals
do not guarantee matching coverage.

137
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:50,220
Anchoring at zero-zero happens to give a valid
fan for this particular L.

138
00:09:50,740 --> 00:09:53,580
Concave shapes and anchor choices differ.

139
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:56,800
Use an algorithm with validated input
conditions;

140
00:09:56,945 --> 00:10:01,620
an arbitrary fan anchor is not safe for
general concave polygons.

141
00:10:02,270 --> 00:10:05,390
An ear is an interior triangle formed by three
consecutive boundary vertices,

142
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:10,630
with a convex middle candidate.

143
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:13,650
Its diagonal must lie inside and other
remaining vertices

144
00:10:13,990 --> 00:10:18,510
must not occupy its interior or disallowed
boundary.

145
00:10:18,635 --> 00:10:25,730
Our first L ear is zero-zero, four-zero,
four-one, of area two.

146
00:10:25,990 --> 00:10:31,550
Store that triangle and remove its middle
vertex four-zero from the remaining contour.

147
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:35,250
The new edge runs from zero-zero to four-one;

148
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:39,150
the removed ear and remainder together cover
the original L.

149
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:42,990
Preserve original vertex indices while
updating

150
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:46,330
contour links and output triangles separately.

151
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:53,680
Observe ice terrain and narrow empty passages
during real Noita movement.

152
00:10:53,900 --> 00:11:00,600
A partition should preserve land while leaving
empty passages unfilled.

153
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:04,740
Recheck neighbors after every added triangle;

154
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:08,200
the original adjacency does not remain
unchanged.

155
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:11,000
Complexity does not change the requirements:

156
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,440
nonoverlapping coverage and the same outer
contour.

157
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:19,640
We observe actual motion without claiming to
convert the pixel terrain into a mesh.

158
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:23,380
Our next diagram removes the remaining ears
and

159
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:26,200
verifies four triangles covering the L of area
seven.

160
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:32,310
After the first ear, triangle zero-two-three
has area one point five.

161
00:11:32,430 --> 00:11:38,610
Remove vertex two; zero-three-four becomes the
next ear, again of area one point five.

162
00:11:38,870 --> 00:11:44,850
The final triangle zero-four-five has area
two; the four areas sum to seven.

163
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:48,410
Also verify nonoverlapping interiors,

164
00:11:48,410 --> 00:11:50,770
unfilled empty space, and coverage of the
original boundary.

165
00:11:51,030 --> 00:11:55,970
Failure to find an ear can indicate invalid
input,

166
00:11:55,970 --> 00:11:59,490
degeneracy or tolerance trouble. Do not loop
forever or emit arbitrary triangles.

167
00:11:59,810 --> 00:12:03,030
Bridging holes needs additional rules;

168
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:08,030
our simple example does not establish a
solution for all holes or intersections.

169
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:11,660
Noita traversal alternates wide areas and
narrow gaps,

170
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:16,220
where close points coexist with long
boundaries.

171
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:20,640
A triangle representation of such outlines can
produce very thin elements.

172
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:23,880
A thin triangle is not automatically invalid,

173
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:28,660
but long edges with small area can increase
numerical sensitivity.

174
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,880
A fixed tolerance does not fit every
coordinate scale or resolution.

175
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:38,380
Removing vertices for simplification can
change passage width and boundaries;

176
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:41,740
preserve required information.

177
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:45,380
We will distinguish a valid partition from a
higher-quality one.

178
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:49,280
Preserve the region before improving element
shapes.

179
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:54,410
Compare a nearly zero-area thin triangle with
a well-shaped one;

180
00:12:54,710 --> 00:12:57,490
small angles can amplify sensitivity.

181
00:12:57,590 --> 00:13:02,210
Among valid ears, quality heuristics may
prefer a larger minimum angle.

182
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:06,110
A favorable angle does not justify a triangle

183
00:13:06,430 --> 00:13:08,350
outside the boundary. Ear validity comes
first.

184
00:13:08,490 --> 00:13:13,910
The same triangle count can have different
shapes depending on diagonal choices.

185
00:13:14,050 --> 00:13:17,850
Recheck winding, indices, area,

186
00:13:17,850 --> 00:13:19,890
coverage and required attribute connectivity
after partitioning.

187
00:13:20,027 --> 00:13:23,930
Use scale-aware length and area tolerances
without deleting

188
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:28,530
required boundaries or holes to improve
appearance.

189
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,520
Observe connected Dorfromantik tiles: one tile
and the whole land have different outlines.

190
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:39,840
Define the center purpose, then check boundary
order, convexity, notches and holes.

191
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:41,600
Partition under suitable conditions and verify
that

192
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:44,820
empty space and the original region are
preserved.

193
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:49,780
Our final practice checks the three centers
and four L triangles.

194
00:13:50,250 --> 00:13:53,490
First, distinguish centroid,

195
00:13:53,490 --> 00:13:57,050
incenter and circumcenter for zero-zero,
four-zero, zero-three.

196
00:13:57,270 --> 00:14:03,810
The centroid is four thirds-one, incenter
one-one, circumcenter two-one point five.

197
00:14:03,970 --> 00:14:07,510
The inradius is one and circumradius two point
five;

198
00:14:07,510 --> 00:14:09,830
neither their centers nor their radii
coincide.

199
00:14:09,990 --> 00:14:16,390
Second, the six-vertex L has four valid
triangles with areas two,

200
00:14:16,390 --> 00:14:22,730
one point five, one point five and two,
totaling seven.

201
00:14:23,190 --> 00:14:27,550
Also check overlap, empty regions and
boundaries.

202
00:14:27,990 --> 00:14:30,530
Validate the complete path from input
conditions to output.

203
00:14:31,130 --> 00:14:35,350
Geometry connected representations, bounds,
planes, coordinates, centers and partitions.

204
00:14:35,970 --> 00:14:41,050
Rendering next converts this3D information
into screen pixels.
